;; L5/W3 fence #2 — `sleep-ms` is VIRTUAL time in sim.
;;
;; Run under the boundary contract (`sim_probe file`), so the whole program is
;; the body of ONE root task. `sleep-ms` from a task in sim is no longer a
;; `thread::sleep`: it arms a one-shot chan on the shared timer service and
;; takes from it (`park_tick`'s shape), so the task PARKS, the shard goes
;; idle, and the advance rule jumps virtual time onto the deadline.
;;
;; Two 30-second sleeps, deliberately SEQUENTIAL (the root sleeps first, and
;; only then spawns the worker) so the expected total is unambiguous: 60 000
;; virtual milliseconds. Both shapes are covered — one sleep in the root task,
;; one in an ordinary `go` block. The wall clock must barely move; in real
;; mode this same program takes a full minute, which is why the test driving
;; this file only runs it under sim.
(do
  (sleep-ms 30000)
  (let [worker (go (do (sleep-ms 30000) :worker-slept))]
    [:root-slept (<!! worker)]))
